+/*
+ * How often the renderer may stop the GPU to collect occlusion results.
+ *
+ * Measured on a Vision Pro, 2026-11-01: this function fired about 980
+ * times a second in Halo's Pillar of Autumn, roughly 196 full pipeline
+ * drains per frame, against less than one per frame in the second level.
+ * It accounted for the entire difference between 5 fps and 33 fps. The
+ * command FIFO goes briefly idle hundreds of times a frame in a scene
+ * full of occluders, and every time it did, everything stopped.
+ *
+ * Deferring is safe for correctness because each drain processes the
+ * whole report queue, so batching writes the same values in the same
+ * order, only later. It is not safe for the query pool, which is a fixed
+ * 1024 entries and asserts on overflow in begin_query, so pool pressure
+ * overrides the limit. A deep report queue does too, since that means
+ * the guest is queueing work rather than waiting on one result.
+ */
+/*
+ * Tuned 2026-11-01 after the first measurement. At 8 ms and a queue of
+ * 64 the level went from 5 fps to a median of 26, and neither valve was
+ * close to firing: queries in flight peaked at 32 of 1024, three
+ * percent of the pool. Average queue depth at drain time was 45 to 52
+ * against a threshold of 64, which says the queue was the binding
+ * limit rather than the clock, so both move.
+ *
+ * The floor that makes this safe to push: every flip calls
+ * pgraph_vk_finish, and every finish processes the whole report queue,
+ * so a report can never be delayed more than one frame no matter what
+ * these numbers say.
+ *
+ * Tuned again 2026-11-04, and this time the instrumentation named the
+ * culprit as this code. In Halo at 30 fps the counters read:
+ *
+ *   gpu drains in 5 s: 380 vtxbuf=49 flip_stall=163 stalled=165
+ *   reports: 165 drains, 5500 skipped, avg queue 72
+ *   drain trigger: clock 165, queue 0, pool 0
+ *
+ * Every drain came from the clock. Neither valve fired once: the queue
+ * averaged 72 against a threshold of 256, and queries in flight never
+ * passed 80 of 1024. So the 16 ms limiter was not holding anything back,
+ * it was the only thing still asking, about 33 times a second, which is
+ * once per frame.
+ *
+ * And once per frame is already free. The floor above says every flip
+ * finishes and every finish empties the queue, so the flip was going to
+ * write those same reports 33 ms later at no cost. The clock drain was
+ * buying nothing and paying a full fence wait for it.
+ *
+ * Two changes follow. The timestamp moves into the internal processor so
+ * it measures when reports were last written rather than when this
+ * function last asked, which means the free per-flip service resets it.
+ * And 16 ms becomes 100 ms, chosen from the data: the worst frame
+ * measured in that session was 80 ms (63 flips in 5 s), and a breaker
+ * shorter than the slowest frame fires precisely during the frames that
+ * are already slow.
+ *
+ * What is left is a deadlock breaker, not a rate limit. It exists for a
+ * guest that spin-reads a report without ever flipping, where neither
+ * valve would trip because one outstanding query grows neither the queue
+ * nor the pool. Worst case there is 100 ms of added latency on that read,
+ * not a hang, so this is a latency choice and not a correctness one.
+ */
+/*
+ * Corrected 2026-11-05, and this one was a plain logic error. There used
+ * to be a second valve here: drain early if the report queue is deeper
+ * than 256, on the reasoning that a deep queue means a lot of outstanding
+ * work. Timing the drains showed it firing 100 to 127 times per 5 s in
+ * heavy combat and costing 1656 to 2408 ms of that 5 s, between a third
+ * and a half of the entire GPU stall budget, while the pool valve never
+ * fired once and queries in flight never passed 128 of 1024.
+ *
+ * The reasoning was backwards. A guest spinning on one result has a queue
+ * one deep and must be served; a guest with 300 queued is firing and
+ * forgetting and is not waiting for any of them. So a deep queue is a
+ * reason to defer, not a reason to drain. It was arguing for urgency from
+ * the evidence of no urgency.
+ *
+ * What remains is the query pool, which is a fixed 1024 and asserts on
+ * overflow, so that valve is a real limit rather than a guess; and the
+ * breaker below, for a guest that spin reads without ever flipping. The
+ * floor is unchanged: every flip finishes and every finish empties the
+ * whole queue.
